  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["R6", "R9"],
      "pins": ["R6.1", "R6.2", "R9.1", "R9.2", "J1.2", "J1.3"],
      "nets": ["FLOAT_HIGH", "FLOAT_LOW", "+3V3", "GND", "/BPUMP"],
      "observed": "On the 'Water Level Sensor/Switches' sheet, R6 (10 k) is wired between +3V3 and the upper float line (J1 pin 2 = FLOAT_HIGH), and R9 (10 k) is wired between GND and the lower float line (J1 pin 3 = FLOAT_LOW). The float switches SW1/SW2 tie FLOAT_HIGH to VCC and FLOAT_LOW to GND, so with these pull assignments each float node is held at the same rail whether its switch is open or closed, and /BPUMP (reached from FLOAT_LOW through R2) is held low at power-up.",
      "expected": "The specification's reference netlist maps the pulls the other way around: 'R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k' (and R2 FLOAT_LOW /BPUMP, R11 FLOAT_HIGH /BATO). With R6 pulling FLOAT_LOW up and R9 pulling FLOAT_HIGH down, the switches can actually change the node levels and /BPUMP defaults high, keeping the main-pump MOSFET T2 off until commanded.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Two float switches are read as binary_sensor GPIO inputs ... (with 10 kΩ/4.7 kΩ pull resistors visible in the .cir file: R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k, R2 FLOAT_LOW /BPUMP 4.7k, R11 FLOAT_HIGH /BATO 4.7k)."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1, 'Water Level Sensor/Switches': R6 10k from +3V3 to the FLOAT_HIGH net (J1 pin 2); R9 10k from GND to the FLOAT_LOW net (J1 pin 3)."}
      ],
      "impact": "Both float-switch inputs are dead (each node is stuck at one rail regardless of water level), so the firmware's low/high water-level binary sensors and the hardware pump/valve interlocks never see a transition. Because FLOAT_LOW is pulled to GND, /BPUMP is low, T1 is off and the R5 pull-up turns the main circulation pump (T2) ON at power-up, defeating the low-level dry-run cutoff.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["PR1", "U3", "U4", "M1A"],
      "pins": ["PR1.5", "PR1.6", "H1.5", "U3.8", "U4.8", "M1A.IO6"],
      "nets": ["EZO_PDIS", "I2C_SDA", "I2C_SCL"],
      "observed": "On sheet 1 the LiDAR PR1 is wired through header H1: its SDA/SCL pins join the main I2C_SDA/I2C_SCL nets, and its TX pin (5) is tied to the EZO_PDIS net while RX (6) is tied to GND. EZO_PDIS is the single-wire power-disable control driven by ESP32 IO6 and connected to the PDIS pins of both ADM3260 isolators (U3.8, U4.8).",
      "expected": "The specification states PR1 is not implemented in this design ('all four pins ... typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net'), and EZO_PDIS is intended to be driven solely by the MCU to power-cycle the pH/EC isolators. A sensor TX output must not be placed on that control net.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the r2 netlist shows PR1 with on_board: no ... and all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1, 'Water Level Sensor/Switches': PR1 LiDAR pin 5 (TX) wired via H1 pin 5 to the EZO_PDIS net; pin 6 (RX) to GND."}
      ],
      "impact": "The LiDAR's TX output fights the ESP32 driver on IO6 and the isolator PDIS pins, so the pH/EC isolated I2C bridges can be randomly enabled/disabled by the LiDAR's serial output, corrupting pH/EC sensing. (Lower confidence: depends on PR1 actually being populated in this build.)",
      "confidence": 0.3
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended design in the float-switch pull network: R6 and R9 are connected to the opposite float nets from the specification's mapping (R6 pull-up on FLOAT_HIGH, R9 pull-down on FLOAT_LOW), which freezes both level inputs and leaves the main pump turned on by default at power-up; a secondary, lower-confidence issue is the LiDAR PR1 being wired onto the I2C bus with its TX tied to the EZO_PDIS control net, which the spec says should be unwired."
}

